# My Training
The majority of my training, 75%, is informed by the principles of ecological psychology. This largely means that I use positional sparring with laser focused goals and constraints to create opportunities for both players to solve problems in real time against real resistance. Some reasons that I chose this approach:

* Requires less procedural knowledge to create effective training sessions.
* Maximizes hands-on time with the core problems of grappling
* Higher fidelity than purely drilling techniques
    - Drilling, both solo and partnered, is still an important part of skill development.
* Ability to intentionally adjust cognitive and physical load to participants.
* Intersection of maximal skill development and conditioning work in a single hour block.

Why only 75%? I could easily train this way in all of my sessions, but it is not worth it to me. Games build the canvas, direct instruction coupled with drilling provide the primary colors, and then more open rolling allows me to blend those core elements into an expression of the art that is uniquely mine. I jokingly call it "Junk Jitsu". 

## Is This Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu?
No. I can't let my desire for recognition and progress compromise my integrity. My training incorporates aspects of Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu, Luta Livre, Judo, Greco-Roman Wrestling, and constrained brawling. The quote that most accurately describes my approach can be found in the Heart Sutra:

> Form is no other than emptiness;\
> emptiness no other than form.

This quote implies that all things are interdependent by nature and lack an inherent independent existence. My approach focuses on the relationship between problems and effective solutions rather than techniques and their geneaology. 

## Positional Paradigm
Jiu Jitsu, in its modern context, carries a positional paradigm largely driven by the scoring positions in the IBJJF ruleset. Scoring conditions are:

| Points | Condition     | Description                                                                       |
|--------|---------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| 4      | Back Control  | Chest to back pin with hooks in.                                                  |
| 4      | Mount         | Sitting on your opponent's torso.                                                 |
| 3      | Guard Pass    | Getting past your opponent's legs to access their hips or torso.                  |
| 2      | Takedown      | Taking your opponent from standing to the ground and maintaining control.         |
| 2      | Sweep         | Reversing your position from bottom to top from a guard position using your legs. |
| 2      | Knee on Belly | Placing your knee or shin on a supine opponent's torso.                           |

### Six Major Positions
* Standing
* Guard(closed, open, half, butterfly, etc.)
* Side Control
* Knee on Belly
* Back Control (including belly down back mount)
* Turtle

### Positions from Best to Worst
| Best    | Controlling opponent's back  |
|---------|------------------------------|
|         | Mounted on opponent          |
|         | Knee on belly on opponent    |
|         | Side control on opponent     |
|         | Opponent turtled Beneath you |
| Neutral | In opponent's guard          |
| Neutral | Opponent in your guard       |
|         | Turtled underneath opponent  |
|         | Opponent has side control    |
|         | Opponent has knee on belly   |
|         | Opponent has mount           |
| Worst   | Opponent has back control    |

These are the major hubs in a grappling encounter from a Jiu Jitsu lens. Each is dynamic and constantly evolving rather than a static position.The liminal space between the positions is just as important as the positions themselves as scrambles can provide opportunities for quickly changing the paradigm of an encounter by turning defensive grappling into opportunities for progression and counter attack. 

## Submissions
Bend it how it shouldn't or compress it.

Submissions can be broken down into three categories each with their own branches:

* Joint locks
    - Straight
    - Twisting
* Compression
    - Strangles (blood restriction)
    - Chokes (breath restriction)
    - Slicers (muscle compression against a wedge)
* Other Mangles
    - Anything that does not fit cleanly into joint locks and compression submissions.
    - Spinal cranks
    - Crotch Rippers
    - Mandible cranks
        

